---
name: harklight-agent
version: "0.2.3"
description: "Send and receive Harklight conversations on a self-hosted server, enroll a device when authorized, use separately granted agent search and response tools, or construct supported web-client entry links."
---

# Harklight for self-hosted agents

Use this skill with a self-hosted Harklight Server. Ask its operator for the
server address, certificate hostname, public CA certificate, person account,
existing enrolled device directory and allowed sender ID. Verify the CA through
the operator's trusted channel; network reachability alone establishes no
account trust. Resolve missing configuration with that operator rather than
copying private keys, inventing endpoint ports or changing grants to make a
send succeed. Installing this skill grants no credentials or messaging authority.

Agent conversations use the separately released `harklight-agent` CLI described
below. The server package does not bundle that executable. For enrollment and
registered-agent tools, set `PACKAGE` to the extracted package directory and
`HARKLIGHT_SERVER_CLI` to its `bin/harklight`; these tools do not start a second server. The package's
`SELF_HOST.md` explains server setup and port exposure. A self-hosted Server may
publish `none` as its file upload cap in `GET /v2/me`; use its published limits.

## Links to the web client

Obtain the verified browser origin, environment (self-hosted, hosted production
or preview), installed web-client version, availability and sign-in prerequisites
from the operator's existing deployment information. For self-hosted setup, use
the package's `SELF_HOST.md`. No portable web-base or availability discovery
interface has been verified for agents; report missing information rather than
inventing a configuration field or discovery endpoint. The device port and
device enrollment do not supply a browser address or browser session. Do not
derive availability from a hostname or source merge. Keep instance values in
existing deployment configuration, not in this portable skill.

For a compatible self-hosted package, the supported entry points are `/inbox`
and `/settings` at that browser origin. Use `/inbox` as the ordinary fallback
when no supported link to a particular item exists. `/app/` serves the bundled
client, but is not a message or conversation route. Server routes are rooted at
`/`; a reverse-proxy subpath is not established by this contract. Use the exact
paths without a trailing slash rather than assuming the server accepts the
client parser's looser spelling.

For example, given the synthetic browser origin `https://inbox.example.test:9443`,
the entry links are `https://inbox.example.test:9443/inbox` and
`https://inbox.example.test:9443/settings`. Construct these with a URL resolver
using the verified origin and fixed path, preserving its scheme, host and port.
These routes take no message, sender, person, recipient or session identifiers.
Do not append an identifier as a path segment, query parameter or fragment: the
bundled client's selected message and chat state live in browser history state,
not a shareable URL. Query parameters are ignored; there is no return-to parameter. A sender/message pair such as `app:example` and `request-1`
can identify an item in an accompanying explanation, but cannot select it through
these entry links.

There is no identifier escaping recipe for an unsupported deep link. Leave query
and fragment empty for ordinary entry links, and escape the complete URL for its
output context if embedding it in HTML. Do not carry credentials, invitation or
sign-in tokens into a constructed link. Do not add tester-only preview controls
to agent links.

A self-hosted browser without a valid session gets sign-in help (HTTP 401) at
these pages; `/sign-in` explains the existing owner-issued sign-in-link route.
Use that authorized sign-in procedure rather than constructing a token. The
sign-in exchange returns to `/inbox`, not a selected message. Device credentials
and an agent's send permission do not sign the human into the browser.

These rules describe the Server-bundled client, not every hosted deployment or
future Web revision. A working hosted account/sign-in page does not establish
that its Web client is enabled; a coming-soon page means it is not available to
customers. Use the hosted operator's verified sign-in route, not the self-hosted
`/sign-in` path. Check the instance's recorded release/configuration before using
the entry links: merged source, a published package, an installed package and an
available hosted web client are distinct states. A missing or disabled web client
is not repaired by changing the URL. Preserve the supported conversation CLI
route below when web navigation is unavailable; a link or opened page proves
neither delivery, receipt nor a human reply and does not transfer conversation
custody or start a listener.

## Explicitly authorized enrollment

Use an existing enrollment for ordinary sends. If asked to enroll a new device,
the server package's headless CLI creates its identity locally. The person owner
approves the displayed code and public-key fingerprint for the intended person
and label. The owner can grant a sender pattern during approval with `--senders`
or later with `owner grant-sender --device FINGERPRINT --sender PATTERN`.
Omit sender grants for a read/respond-only device. Approval does not make the
device an owner. Sender-pattern matching is defined in the shared core below.

For example, on the agent's machine, with values supplied by the operator:

```sh
"$PACKAGE/bin/harklight" enroll start --directory "$DEVICE_DIR" \
  --connect "$SERVER_ADDRESS" --name "$SERVER_NAME" --port "$ENROLL_PORT" \
  --ca "$CA_PEM" --label "Script sender"
```

Give the approval code to the authenticated owner. The owner checks the
intended person, label and key fingerprint before running `bin/harklight owner
approve` with `--senders app:example` for this sender. For an explicitly
authorized enrollment, check the saved request with the installed enrollment
command. For an approved request it proves current key access with one
authenticated client API v2 inbox read on the device port. This does not prove
that an agent session receives its messages:

```sh
"$PACKAGE/bin/harklight" enroll status --directory "$DEVICE_DIR" \
  --connect "$SERVER_ADDRESS" --name "$SERVER_NAME" --port "$ENROLL_PORT" \
  --device-port "$DEVICE_PORT" --ca "$CA_PEM"
```

Keep the directory if a submission reply is lost. `enroll retry` uses the same
local key; a `key_pending` result does not recover the old code or prove
enrollment. Follow the command's expiry guidance, and treat `key_in_use` or an
unknown status handle as unresolved until the owner reconciles it. This
headless enrollment procedure is distinct from the native app's guided device
joining.

Use the `harklight-agent` CLI for agent messages and their replies, with the
originating enrolled identity and journal. There is no device-send or receipt
fallback. If the originating session cannot keep a listener, use the one-shot
`read --session HANDLE` procedure in the shared core when the originating work
resumes. Serving a person turn or answer records it as Delivered on the server;
the handoff receipt is a separate `receipt` command.

For a direct sent-message lookup, Agent 0.2.0 supports
`harklight-agent message-get --item-id ID [--revision N]`, or
`--sender-id S --message-id M` for a message accepted before the cutover. It
reads the latest version by default or the selected retained revision; it does
not mark Seen. `not_found` for a selected revision means the requested revision
is gone. The shared core below gives the command examples.

The enrolled-device interface remains available for existing inbox work:
`device read` takes a client API v2 inbox snapshot or change batch and saves it
with its cursor before advancing that cursor; there is no ACK.
`device respond --sender ID --message-id ID --answer answer.json` deliberately
submits an answer object (at most one of `choice`, `comment` or `result`, or
`{}` for no comment) to a message already stored by the server, saving its exact
body and `Idempotency-Key` before sending. These commands do not create new
messages or retrieve an origin receipt. Use the separate `harklight-agent` CLI
for new agent messages and replies.
